**Verdict: correct-with-fixes.** Claim A is correct, the finite-group enumeration is correct, and both auxiliary replacements are valid sufficient conditions. The fixes concern an unstated normalization in Claim B and making the resolution requirements explicit.

**Claim A.** The fixed-point set is closed under completing pairs, so its pointwise fixed blocks number
\[
N_3=\frac{f(f-1)}6.
\]
For \(f=1\), this is zero. Every transposition pair has a fixed completing point: applying \(g\) to its unique block fixes that block and therefore its third point. Distinct transpositions produce distinct blocks. Thus
\[
N_1=\frac{v-f}{2},
\]
and these exhaust the other setwise fixed blocks.

Because \(g\) permutes the chosen parallel classes, a fixed block’s unique containing class is fixed. Conversely, in a fixed class, the unique block containing any fixed point is fixed. Each fixed class consequently contributes exactly \(f\) fixed-point incidences, giving
\[
cf=3N_3+N_1
=\frac{f(f-1)}2+\frac{v-f}2.
\]
Therefore
\[
2cf=f^2-2f+v,
\qquad
v=f(2c-f+2),
\]
so \(f\mid v\).

The resolution hypothesis is essential to this argument. Replace “No conclusion without an invariant chosen resolution” with the more precise statement: **this divisibility conclusion is not established from block-set invariance alone.**

**Claim B.** Here “transversal” must mean exactly one representative from every orbit of triples under finite translations, which fix both infinities individually.

Under that assumption, the negation argument works. For a block
\[
B=\{\infty_j,x,y\},
\]
negation sends it to the translate of \(B\) by \(-x-y\). Thus \(B\) and \(-B\) belong to the same translation orbit. Transversality and block-set invariance force \(B=-B\). Since the finite modulus is odd, its finite pair must be \(\{x,-x\}\), with \(x\ne0\).

Each infinity has exactly \((n-1)/2\) blocks containing two finite points. Hence both infinities use every nonzero antipodal pair, repeating finite pairs and contradicting the STS condition. **Transversality is required here; resolution invariance is not.**

The CRT computations are:

| Modulus | Unit group | Order; exponent | Nonidentity involutions and total fixed-point counts |
|---|---|---|---|
| \(55\) | \(C_4\times C_{10}\) | \(40;\ 20\) | \(21:7,\quad34:13,\quad54:3\) |
| \(85\) | \(C_4\times C_{16}\) | \(64;\ 16\) | \(16:7,\quad69:19,\quad84:3\) |

Indeed, the number of finite fixed points is \(\gcd(a-1,n)\). The relevant gcds are respectively \(5,11,1\) and \(5,17,1\). Thus Claim A excludes precisely the stated non-negation involutions. Every even-order multiplier has an involutory power, which also preserves the block set and chosen resolution.

Modulo \(55\), the only remaining possible nontrivial odd order is \(5\). Such an element is identity modulo \(5\) and has order \(5\) modulo \(11\), giving seven fixed points altogether and only 1- and 5-cycles on points.

The small fix is that **\(\{0,\infty_1,\infty_2\}\) need not be a block unless the base has been normalized.** Instead, use the unique block containing the infinity pair:
\[
B_\infty=\{r,\infty_1,\infty_2\}.
\]
It is setwise fixed, so its parallel class is fixed. In that class, every block meeting the fixed-point set is fixed. A fixed triple under a permutation with only 1- and 5-cycles consists entirely of fixed points. Consequently, the seven fixed points would be partitioned into triples, an impossibility.

This repairs the argument without needing normalization. Modulo \(85\), the unit group is a 2-group, so the involution argument already exhausts all nonidentity units.

